    <body>
      <Value><![CDATA[## The Decision

When I was building out the blog page schema, I faced a classic decision: should blog posts be edited in a rich text editor (like Tiptap) or in Markdown? Both have their merits, and both are available in Umbraco. But after thinking through the editing experience, the rendering pipeline, and what makes sense for a blogging platform, I chose Markdown.

Here's why.

## Markdown Keeps Editors Focused

Markdown is gloriously simple. There are no floating toolbars, no dialogs for link attributes, no temptation to spend three minutes tweaking font sizes. You write content, you format it with a few intuitive symbols (bold, headings, quotes), and you move on. The cognitive load is minimal.

When I render the Markdown property in Razor, Umbraco handles the conversion to HTML automatically. No Markdig. No external dependencies. Just built-in magic. That simplicity is exactly what a focused blogging platform needs.

## It Plays Nicely with Razor

Rich text editors like Tiptap return complex JSON structures that require careful deserialization in Razor. Markdown, on the other hand, arrives as plain HTML thanks to Umbraco's built-in processing. That means I can render it with a straightforward Html.Raw and trust that it's safe and correct.

The rendering pattern is clean: retrieve the Markdown as a string, render it as raw HTML. No special utilities, no conditional checks for null values. It just works.

## Consistency and Predictability

One thing I learned while building this site is that predictability matters. With Markdown, what you write is what you get. An author can't accidentally apply conflicting styles or create weird nesting scenarios that break the layout. The format enforces a certain discipline that actually helps content shine.

Plus, Markdown is portable. If an editor wants to grab their post content and use it elsewhere - a static site generator, a different CMS, plain text files - they can do it without conversion headaches.

## The Trade-Off

I won't pretend Markdown is perfect for every use case. Some editors prefer the immediacy of visual formatting. But for a blogging site that values clarity, focus, and simplicity - especially one that's being built live on stage - Markdown wins.

So here we are: the Blog page leans on Markdown, the Razor template keeps things lean, and editors can concentrate on what matters: writing great content.]]></Value>
    </body>
    <excerpt>
      <Value><![CDATA[I chose Markdown for the blog post editor because it keeps content creation focused, integrates seamlessly with Umbraco's built-in HTML rendering, and lets writers concentrate on what matters most: the words themselves.]]></Value>
    </excerpt>
